Sum simplification isolation checks
===================================

  ./maxima-local --no-init --lisp=sbcl \
    --batch-string='run_testsuite(tests=[rtest_parallel_summation,rtestsum]);'

Run every configured Lisp. Require all 17 new examples and the complete
requested test count; Maxima can catch Lisp errors and still exit zero.

SUMSUM previously shared its computed and unevaluated-term accumulators
between all calls. They now belong to each invocation. Its five internal
helpers are local recursive functions. The coefficient and exponent returned
by SMONO through *A and *N are dynamically bound for this invocation, so
matching also preserves the caller's state. The mathematical formulas and
public SUM behavior are unchanged.

The deterministic re-entry checks temporarily intercept SIMPLIFYA at the
outer call's numeric argument and finish an inner sum before resuming. One
uses a native worker (the serial fallback on a threadless Lisp); another
throws out of an inner sum while the outer already has a computed term.
Both computed and unevaluated terms must stay isolated. The original
simplifier is restored with UNWIND-PROTECT. Run in an otherwise idle Maxima
process; the temporary interception is test instrumentation only.

128 generated rational-coefficient polynomial cases compare against exact
finite addition, independently of the Bernoulli formulas. Another 48 cases
run through ordinary parallel scheduling and the serial fallback. Inputs
must remain unchanged; direct checks retain SUMSUM's one-value unevaluated
and two-value partial/computed results. Examples also cover finite and
infinite geometric sums, binomial sums, partial sums and divergence.

This isolates this summation group's scratch state. It does not certify
all simplifier callbacks, shared caches or the entire parallel evaluator.
